// @check
// @error set: expected f32, got i32
// @error set: expected K, got string
// @error set: expected N, got i32
// @error set: expected f32, got f64
// THE REJECTED SIDE of `m.set(k, v)`'s literal narrowing — what makes the grant a rule
// rather than a hole. Each line's `m[k] = v` twin rejects too (with its own wording),
// which is the point: `.set` runs `assignableExpr`, the very function the assignment
// path runs, so a value the assignment path refuses is refused here as well.
//
//   1. An INEXACT integer literal. 16777217 is the first integer f32 cannot hold, and
//      the rule is exactness for integers (rounding is the contract only when the
//      author writes the `.`) — so this is the reject that keeps `16777216` above from
//      being "any integer fits".
//   2/3. A NON-MEMBER literal against a literal union, string and integer members
//      alike. The union is closed; a fresh member would mint an atom id nothing reads.
//   4. A non-literal f64 VALUE. The grant is about LITERALS, not values: `x` denotes a
//      double whose demotion would be lossy, and no context can make it not be.
type K = "a" | "b"
type N = 0 | 1

const a: {[string]: f32} = Map()
a.set("y", 16777217)

const b: {[string]: K} = Map()
b.set("y", "c")

const c: {[string]: N} = Map()
c.set("y", 7)

const d: {[string]: f32} = Map()
const x: f64 = 0.5
d.set("y", x)
